 @jonathonrosalia9345  that's not true at all. 6600XT is somewhere between a 3060 and a 3060Ti. RT results is generally skewed since benchmarks are often set  to ultra settings. Radeon cards share rasterization performance with RT performance so once you lower settings to high you level out RT performance difference without much loss to visuals quality.
Nvidia marketing is extremely strong this generation. And there is a lot of nvidia fanboism out there benchmarking with DLSS on nvidia cards. And there's a big problem with that. 1.Far from all games have DLSS. 2. DLSS importance will die off with time (proprietary tech always do it's not in developers interest to reach out to specific hardware, they want all gamers to be able to play) 3.AMD users can use other smart techniques to improve their performance, FSR (and future RSR, which looks great and perceptually comparable at 1440 Ultra Quality and above) Radeon boost (which is never spoken of). Lowers resolution when turning, as you don't notice resolution loss in those cases, and then it increases resolution when moving straight or standing still. Where you need quality instead of framerate. Radeon sharpening, which beats nvidia sharpening and makes TAA games look like they increases resolution by a whole step. 1080 looks more or less like 1440p at 80% sharpness.

I was very pro-nvidia until I got a Radeon card myself then I was like "why don't people cover this!?". I think people are easily affected by tech tubers and nvidias strong marketing. AMD made great cards this generation. Which offers way more value for its market price.

 @lordv1le859  FSR is a bit mediocre at the moment. The framerate boost is finally on par with DLSS, but there are fewer games that support it and it uses a more basic spatial upscaling methodology. 

RSR may even the playing field for AMD by being applicable in every game, but it will only be supported on its own 6XXX gen cards and will still be a spatial solution. 

What might really help older cards is Intel  bringing XeSS (with "AI", matrix based upscaling algorithms without needing tensor cores), but they aren't arriving anytime soon and we have no idea how efficient the upscaling will be.

 @lordv1le859  note I said "stone age". Ceramics are a modern invention. 

It's a perfect analogy and your comment only serves to strengthen it: newer technologies are generally better than older, less sophisticated ones. Ceramics as a modern material beats traditional metal, which in turn beats stone age tools. 

Spatial up scaling is an older, less sophisticated and less effective technology than super sampling

